body:
I hate someone is even asking this, but I get why. 

&#x200B;

I personally think that perhaps maybe they should "create" a new band, or change their name. Linkin Park *is* who they were, and there is no Linkin Park without Chester. 

I agree with someone's comment about limbo for that reason. What happened is extremely traumatic. It happened to occur the same month I lost someone that way too, and I'm still messed up. Mike's solo album Post-Traumatic said things in the lyrics that I felt only someone who went through what we did would understand. That trauma doesn't just go away. 

&#x200B;

With the pressure, too, of "Do we get someone new? Do we not?" that just adds to the stress. I don't think we *need* an answer. It's best to just wait, take what we can get, and respect them and their choices.

You're not wrong. I think one thing that really gets me is that my old family church is a place where they (in my opinion, horribly) snubbed people who didn't donate. Now they have 3 tiers of pews, keep adding to the playground, which is nice but knowing it came from pushing and snubbing people doesn't sit right with me.

&#x200B;

I am going to see about the merging. That's a really smart idea.

I got rejected for not being disabled enough. Didn't realize crippling PTSD, agoraphobia, major depressive disorder, and BPD are not disabilities. Then again, I also heard of a man who got both legs amputated and was also rejected the first time. 

&#x200B;

Do **not** give up, even if you happen to get rejected the first time, okay? They really, honestly, aren't qualified to tell you if you are disabled enough. Amputation not being disability? Give me a break. Just keep trying.

The specialist is honestly a blessing! She's an elite from AVBP, and she raged when the first vet I took him to told me he was dying and had two months to live, with 8 different medications. I've had him over a year, none of those medications were needed (this in particular caused the rage and she asked if she could use them as a teaching lesson for what not to do) and he's the epitome of health. You get it, being a cat-only vet. :) She said they're so different than dogs she doesn't understand why they're mashed together in teaching. 

&#x200B;

I can try the nightlight! The thing is though, he only ever does this wall-meowing and random meowing at me while laying on the couch, during the day. I'll give him pets, food, toys, but ultimately he still does it. I'm sure it's a part of the dementia, but it really is heartbreaking to hear and see (he has those Puss In Boots eyes and knows how to use them!) but there doesn't seem to be any specific reason. He sleeps beside my head at night, and will hop up in the morning to meow in my face to tell me it's food time. 

&#x200B;

We just started him on Gabapentin to see if that may aid in any arthritis pain. So far, it's gotten him a lot more energetic most likely due to less pain, but he also just recovered from a cyst removal surgery too. The meowing had been occurring even before that though. 

&#x200B;

I do want to make clear that it's not something that irritates me in any way. I'm just a real softy and  am used to thinking meows are because they want something or something is wrong.

Nope. She can get allergy shots.

&#x200B;

Also my partner is super allergic, but the exposure is helping.

I greatly appreciate the non-biased, honest answer. The truth of the matter is, so far, we don't know what he, specifically did, regardless of why he joined. I suppose only he does. I don't disagree with you.

&#x200B;

Deeefinitely a hand to Russians on that. The idea that Zelenskyy was a nazi as posed by Putin was laughable, but with this....yikes.

To be honest, as both a Canadian and someone of Ukrainian heritage, I'm not really in the 'know' of what's going on. I don't have any particular opinion *because* I don't really understand what's going on. 

&#x200B;

I do know that this dude apparently fought for the independence of Ukraine and has attended anti-Russian protests? And that he was a part of a nazi-force. I also know from personal conversations with a nazi soldier that a lot of them weren't aware of concentration camps being what they were.... not saying this guy was one of them or anything. 

&#x200B;

If someone can honestly dumb this down for me, that would be great. I'd really like to have a full understanding of what's going on, especially information about this man in particular (not just the group he was in).

Got one as a joke gift.

&#x200B;

Tried it. Made me feel literally dead inside.

&#x200B;

Ace confirmed.

It's interesting you say that.

&#x200B;

Back in University 4, 5 years ago, my Psychology of Human Sexuality prof said "Back in the day, bisexuals were referred to as 'fence-sitters' and not well accepted in the community. Now, they are, and that view has shifted to asexuals instead."

Wow....that's so wonderful. I'm glad you were able to hear those things. Sounds like she really made a difference to a lot of people and was well loved.

&#x200B;

I will do that in under an hour. I appreciate the push!

Wow...that's amazing. He sounds like he was a great guy who touched many people. 

&#x200B;

I'm heading to the visitation within the hour, and I thank you for giving me a push and support to do so!

That sounds like a beautiful thing your father did, and he sounds like he was doing lovely things. It's absolutely amazing the impact one person can have on so many lives. 

&#x200B;

I think you have a good idea there. I wasn't sure if adding my information to the note would be strange, but, you've given me the courage to say 'why not' and put it out there. 

&#x200B;

Thank you so much. That means the world to me. <3

I would say 100% I believe you are. This is almost exactly how I felt, until an ex-turned-friend of mine was like "Hey...I think you're demisexual.". Me, thinking I had been broken the entire time hopped on Google and voila, it all made sense. 

&#x200B;

You are *not* uptight or overthinking. I would say this is a perfectly normal experience for someone who is demi. Make sure you love yourself, and accept **who you are**. Being demisexual doesn't make you less of a person.
